<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-cutting-the-rational-numbers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Cutting the Rational Numbers
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      So far, our progression through the standard number systems <m> \N \subsetneq \Z \subsetneq \Q </m> has proceeded in order of containment. Every time we have expanded our perspective to consider larger number systems, it has resulted in only the acquisition of new desirable properties. This is an artefact of our focus on the analytical rather than say the number-theoretical; there are for example incredibly interesting properties of the natural numbers <m> \N </m> and the integers <m> \Z </m> which are lost in the jump to the rational numbers <m> \Q </m>. 
    </p>
    
    <p>
      However, we have yet to see any deficiencies with the rational number system <m> \Q </m> which would motivate seeking an extension. These deficiencies exist, and we will see in <xref ref="section-the-complete-ordered-field"/> and later in <xref ref="section-completeness-and-compactness"/> the benefits in working in the <term> real numbers </term> <m> \R </m>. As a brief example, we note that <m> \Q </m> can be written as a particular type of disjoint union <m> \Q = X \sqcup Y </m>, where
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          X \amp = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \lt 0 \textrm{ or } q^{ 2 } \lt 2 }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          Y \amp = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \gt 0 \textrm{ and } q^{ 2 } \gt 2 }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      are defined by strict inequalities. This decomposition of <m> \Q </m> is an example of a <term> Dedekind cut </term>, and we will see that such a decomposition of <m> \R </m> cannot exist.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-dedekind-cut">
    <title>
      Dedekind cut
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      dedekind cut
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \R </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the set of real numbers
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        A proper, non-empty subset <m> \emptyset \subsetneq X \subsetneq \Q </m> of the rational numbers <m> \Q </m> is called a <term> Dedekind cut </term> if it satisfies the following conditions:
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              For all rational numbers <m> q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } \in \Q </m>, if <m> q_{ 1 } \leq q_{ 2 } </m> and <m> q_{ 2 } \in X </m>, then <m> q_{ 1 } \in X </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              <m> X </m> does not have a maximum; that is, for all elements <m> q_{ 1 } \in X </m>, there is an element <m> q_{ 2 } \in X </m> so that <m> q_{ 1 } \lt q_{ 2 } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        
        The set of Dedekind cuts is denoted <m> \R </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <example xml:id="example-dedekind-cuts">
    <title>
      Dedekind cuts
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      rational Dedekind cut
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> X_{ q } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the rational Dedekind cut associated to <m> q </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each rational number <m> q \in \Q </m>, consider the subset
        
        <md>
          X_{ q } = \setmid{ p \in \Q }{ p \lt q }
        </md>
        
        of the rational numbers <m> \Q </m>. We observe that <m> X_{ q } </m> is a proper and non-empty subset of <m> \Q </m>, since it contains <m> q - 1 </m> but not <m> q </m> itself. Moreover, for all rational numbers <m> p_{ 1 } , p_{ 2 } \in \Q </m>, if <m> p_{ 1 } \leq p_{ 2 } </m> and <m> p_{ 2 } \in X_{ q } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          p_{ 1 } \leq p_{ 2 } \lt q
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> p_{ 1 } \in X_{ q } </m>. Finally, for all elements <m> p_{ 1 } \in X </m>, we observe that
        
        <md>
          p \lt p + \frac{ q - p }{ 2 } \lt p + \of{ q - p } = q
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> p + \frac{ q - p }{ 2 } \in X_{ q } </m>. In summary, we have shown that associated to each rational number <m> q \in \Q </m> is a Dedekind cut <m> X_{ q } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    We will see that the above construction realizes the rational numbers <m> \Q </m> as a subset of the set of Dedekind cuts. The Dedekind cuts which arise in this manner will be called <term> rational </term>. We will shortly see that not every Dedekind cut is rational.
  </p>
  
  <lemma xml:id="lemma-rational-dedekind-cuts">
    <title>
      Rational Dedekind cuts
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        A Dedekind cut <m> X </m> is rational if and only if its complement <m> \Q \setminus X </m> has a minimum.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> X </m> be a Dedekind cut, and suppose that <m> X = X_{ q } </m> for some rational number <m> q \in \Q </m>. Note that <m> X = X_{ q } </m> has complement
        
        <md>
          \Q \setminus X = \Q \setminus X_{ q } = \setmid{ p \in \Q }{ p \notin X_{ q } } = \setmid{ p \in \Q }{ p \geq q }
        </md>.
        
        This set contains <m> q </m>, since <m> q \geq q </m>. Moreover, <m> p \geq q </m> for all elements <m> p \in \Q \setminus X_{ q } </m>. Thus <m> \min \of{ \Q \setminus X } = q </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> \Q \setminus X </m> has a minimum element, say <m> q = \min \of{ \Q \setminus X } \in \Q </m>. Let <m> p \in \Q </m>, and suppose that <m> p \geq q </m>. If <m> p \in X </m>, then that would imply that <m> q \in X </m>. This contradiction implies that <m> p \notin X </m>. On the other hand, if <m> p \lt q </m>, then <m> p \notin \Q \setminus X </m>, since <m> \min \of{ \Q \setminus X } = q </m>. Thus
        
        <md>
          X = \setmid{ p \in \Q }{ p \lt q } = X_{ q }
        </md>
        
        is rational.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-irrational-dedekind-cuts">
    <title>
      Irrational Dedekind cuts
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Not all Dedekind cuts are rational.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Consider the subset
        
        <md>
          X = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \lt 0 \textrm{ or } q^{ 2 } \lt 2 }
        </md>
        
        of the rational numbers <m> \Q </m>. We observe that <m> X </m> is a non-empty and proper subset of <m> \Q </m>, since it contains <m> 1 </m> but not <m> 2 </m>. Let <m> p_{ 1 } , p_{ 2 } \in \Q </m> be rational numbers, and suppose that <m> p_{ 1 } \leq p_{ 2 } </m> and <m> p_{ 2 } \in X </m>. If <m> p_{ 2 } \lt 0 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          p_{ 1 } \leq p_{ 2 } \lt 0
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> p_{ 1 } \in X </m>. On the other hand, if <m> p_{ 2 }^{ 2 } \lt 2 </m>, then either <m> p_{ 1 } \lt 0 </m> or <m> 0 \leq p_{ 1 } \leq p_{ 2 } </m>, in which case <m> p_{ 1 }^{ 2 } \leq p_{ 2 }^{ 2 } \lt 2 </m>. In either case, we conclude that <m> p_{ 1 } \in X </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Finally, let <m> q \in X </m>. If <m> q \leq 0 </m>, then let <m> p = 1 </m>, and note that <m> p \in X </m> and <m> q \lt p </m>. On the other hand, suppose that <m> q \gt 0 </m> and <m> q^{ 2 } \lt 2 </m>, and consider the rational number
        
        <md>
          p = \frac{ 2 q + 2 }{ 2 + q } \in \Q
        </md>.
        
        Note that
        
        <md>
          p = \frac{ 2 q + 2 }{ 2 + q } = \frac{ 2 q + q^{ 2 } }{ 2 + q } + \frac{ 2 - q^{ 2 } }{ 2 + q } = q + \frac{ 2 - q^{ 2 } }{ 2 + q } \gt q
        </md>,
        
        and
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            2 - p^{ 2 } \amp = \frac{ 2 \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } } - \frac{ \of{ 2 q + 2 }^{ 2 } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{ 4 - 2 q^{ 2 } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } } = \frac{ 2 \of{ 2 - q^{ 2 } } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } } \gt 0
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> p^{ 2 } \lt 2 </m>; that is, <m> p \in X </m>. We conclude that <m> X </m> is a Dedekind cut, and so it suffices to show that <m> X </m> is not rational.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        To that end, we note that <m> X </m> has complement
        
        <md>
          \Q \setminus X = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \geq 0 \textrm{ and } q^{ 2 } \geq 2 }
        </md>.
        
        In fact, since <m> q^{ 2 } \neq 2 </m> for all rational numbers <m> q \in \Q </m> (this fact is proven in any introductory number theory course),
        
        <md>
          \Q \setminus X = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \geq 0 \textrm{ and } q^{ 2 } \gt 2 }
        </md>.
        
        We will show that <m> \Q \setminus X </m> has no minimum element. Indeed, let <m> q \in \Q \setminus X </m>, so that <m> q \geq 0 </m> and <m> q^{ 2 } \gt 2 </m>. We again consider the rational number
        
        <md>
          p = \frac{ 2 q + 2 }{ 2 + q } \in \Q
        </md>.
        
        We see that <m> p \geq 0 </m>. Moreover, now that <m> q^{ 2 } \gt 2 </m>, we observe that
        
        <md>
          p = \frac{ 2 q + 2 }{ 2 + q } = \frac{ 2 q + q^{ 2 } }{ 2 + q } + \frac{ 2 - q^{ 2 } }{ 2 + q } = q + \frac{ 2 - q^{ 2 } }{ 2 + q } \lt q
        </md>
        
        and
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            2 - p^{ 2 } \amp = \frac{ 2 \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } } - \frac{ \of{ 2 q + 2 }^{ 2 } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac{ 4 - 2 q^{ 2 } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } } = \frac{ 2 \of{ 2 - q^{ 2 } } }{ \of{ 2 + q }^{ 2 } } \lt 0
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> p^{ 2 } \gt 2 </m>. In particular, <m> p \lt q </m> and <m> p \in \Q \setminus X </m>. Thus <m> \Q \setminus X </m> has no minimum element, and so <xref ref="lemma-rational-dedekind-cuts"/> implies that <m> X </m> is not rational.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <p>
    Once we define the arithmetic of Dedekind cuts, we will see that the Dedekind cut
    
    <md>
      X = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \lt 0 \textrm{ or } q^{ 2 } \lt 2 }
    </md>
    
    from the above proof of <xref ref="proposition-irrational-dedekind-cuts"/> represents the <term> irrational </term> real number <m> \sqrt{ 2 } </m>. First, however, we observe that the rational numbers <m> \Q </m> inject into <m> \R </m>.
  </p>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-rational-numbers-as-real-numbers">
    <title>
      Rational numbers as real numbers
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The map <m> \iota \colon \Q \to \R </m> defined by the formula <m> \iota \of{ q } = X_{ q } </m> is injective.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } \in \Q </m> be rational numbers, and suppose that <m> \iota \of{ q_{ 1 } } = \iota \of{ q_{ 2 } } </m>. As shown in the proof of <xref ref="lemma-rational-dedekind-cuts"/>, <m> \min \of{ X_{ q } } = q </m> for all rational numbers <m> q \in \Q </m>. Therefore,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            q_{ 1 } \amp = \min \of{ X_{ q_{ 1 } } } = \min \of{ \iota \of{ q_{ 1 } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \min \of{ \iota \of{ q_{ 2 } } } = \min \of{ X_{ q_{ 2 } } } = q_{ 2 }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Thus <m> \iota \colon \Q \to \R </m> is injective.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      Going forward, we will often exploit the injection <m> \iota \colon \Q \to \R </m> to pretend that <m> \Q \subseteq \R </m> by identifying a rational number with its associated rational Dedekind cut. We will now construct a total order <m> \leq </m> on <m> \R </m> which extends the standard order on <m> \Q </m>.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
